# Line endings, decided by the repo rather than by whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this, the answer depends on each machine's `core.autocrlf`, which is
# `true` by default on Git for Windows and unset everywhere else. The result is
# that the same file has different content on two developers' disks and a
# whitespace-only diff appears the first time a Linux machine touches a file a
# Windows machine wrote. This repo is meant to be cross-platform, so the rule
# belongs to the repo.
#
# `text=auto` means: detect whether a file is text, and if it is, store it with
# LF in the repository and check it out in the platform's native ending. The
# working tree stays native — Windows still gets CRLF — and the history stays
# LF, so a diff only ever shows a real change.
* text=auto

# Files that must keep LF even on Windows, because something other than an
# editor reads them and a CR would be part of the value or the shebang.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the Windows-native half of the tooling; keep it CRLF.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Binary, stated rather than guessed. Auto-detection is good but these are the
# ones where a wrong guess corrupts the file rather than just annoying someone.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.qcow2     binary
*.img       binary
*.iso       binary

# Text that happens to look binary-ish, stated for the same reason.
*.svg       text
*.md        text
*.json      text

# Licence texts are legal documents: byte-for-byte identical to upstream is a
# property worth keeping, and CRLF conversion would break a checksum comparison
# against the canonical text.
LICENSE      -text
NOTICE       -text
backend/LICENSE -text
backend/NOTICE  -text
